# SECTION 03 — PROBLEM STATEMENT

## Problem Statement

Modern frontend ecosystems provide thousands of libraries and frameworks.

Most organisations select technologies through:

* popularity,
* community trends,
* developer familiarity,
* online comparisons,
* isolated feature evaluation.

These approaches frequently produce:

* inconsistent technology stacks,
* duplicated capability,
* incompatible architectures,
* difficult upgrades,
* fragmented user experience,
* excessive maintenance burden.

Institutional operational platforms require a fundamentally different approach.

The workstation will support:

* multiple terminals,
* hundreds of workspaces,
* thousands of operational components,
* continuous institutional operation,
* multi-monitor environments,
* AI integration,
* complex visualisation,
* real-time streaming,
* collaborative workflows.

Technology decisions made today will influence architectural flexibility for many years.

The COREI Anchor Law requires that the workstation remain scalable across:

* Multi Account
* Multi Broker
* Multi Strategy
* Multi Portfolio
* Multi Asset
* Multi Exchange
* Multi Region
* Multi Team
* Multi Monitor
* Institutional Governance
* 24×7 Operations

Technology therefore becomes a constitutional responsibility rather than merely an engineering preference.

The challenge is not selecting the "best" technology.

The challenge is selecting technologies that collectively strengthen one coherent institutional architecture.

