## Architectural Observation

I believe Phase-05 introduces another long-term COREI engineering principle:

Architecture chooses technology.

Technology never chooses architecture.

This sounds simple, but it prevents one of the biggest causes of architectural drift.

For example, instead of saying:

"Let's use AG Grid because it's powerful."

We ask:

What characteristics must an institutional investigation grid possess?
Can AG Grid satisfy those characteristics?
Can another technology satisfy them better?
Does either technology align with the COREI Constitution, the Operator Architecture, the Operational Environment, and the Experience Architecture?

Only after those questions are answered do we select a technology.

I also think this phase should produce something unique that many architecture documents never include:

A Technology Constitution Matrix.

Every technology should have a formal evaluation record containing:

Capability Domain (Layout, Grid, Charting, Terminal, Graph, AI, etc.)
Architectural Requirements
Candidate Technologies
Evaluation Criteria
Strengths
Weaknesses
Integration Complexity
Performance Characteristics
Scalability Assessment
License & Governance
Institutional Risks
Constitutional Compliance
Final Decision
Reasoning

That matrix will become the authoritative record explaining why every technology exists in COREI, making future upgrades, replacements, and architectural reviews far more disciplined.

