# SECTION 08 — INSTITUTIONAL PERSPECTIVE

## Institutional Perspective

Institutions invest in platforms for decades.

Technology inevitably changes.

Institutional architecture should not.

The Technology Landscape therefore supports continuous technological evolution while preserving operational continuity.

---

## Institutional Governance

Technology adoption shall require institutional review.

No technology enters COREI without documented architectural justification.

---

## Institutional Standardisation

Equivalent capabilities should use common technologies wherever practical.

Avoiding unnecessary duplication improves:

* maintainability,
* consistency,
* operational quality.

---

## Controlled Diversity

Not every capability requires the same technology.

Different architectural responsibilities justify different specialised technologies.

Consistency should never become technological rigidity.

---

## Institutional Knowledge

Every technology decision becomes permanent institutional knowledge.

Future engineers should understand:

* why the technology exists,
* why alternatives were rejected,
* what architectural capability it satisfies,
* when replacement becomes appropriate.

---

## Sustainable Technology Ecosystem

The objective is not assembling the largest technology stack.

The objective is assembling the smallest collection of technologies capable of implementing the complete Institutional Operator Workstation.

Every selected technology should strengthen the ecosystem.

None should increase unnecessary architectural complexity.

The Technology Landscape therefore becomes a governed institutional ecosystem rather than a collection of independent libraries.

