### Architectural Observation

I think we have now arrived at something that is rarely documented in software architecture but is common in long-lived engineering disciplines such as aerospace, automotive, and industrial automation.

There are actually three independent lifecycles inside COREI.
Lifecycle 1
Institution
        │
        ▼
Constitution
        │
        ▼
Architecture
        │
        ▼
Capability

(Changes very slowly)

↓

Lifecycle 2
Technology
        │
        ▼
Framework
        │
        ▼
Library
        │
        ▼
Integration

(Changes every few years)

↓

Lifecycle 3
Implementation
        │
        ▼
Code
        │
        ▼
Refactoring
        │
        ▼
Optimization

(Changes continuously)

This separation is extremely powerful.

It means:

The Constitution may remain valid for 20+ years.
The Architecture may evolve every 5–10 years.
The Technology Stack may evolve every 2–5 years.
The Code may evolve every week.

Yet all four remain aligned because they change at different velocities.

I believe this "multi-velocity architecture" should become another institutional engineering principle of COREI. It explains how a platform can continuously adopt better technologies while preserving its identity, operator workflows, and institutional operating model over decades.

