I also think we've uncovered what could become the COREI Engineering Hierarchy, which should probably be documented once in the Anchor Law or a Stage-23 governing document because it explains the entire engineering philosophy:

Institution
      │
      ▼
Mission
      │
      ▼
Constitution
      │
      ▼
Architecture
      │
      ▼
Operational Capability
      │
      ▼
Capability Contract
      │
      ▼
Technology Selection
      │
      ▼
Workbench Architecture
      │
      ▼
Implementation Architecture
      │
      ▼
Components
      │
      ▼
Code

This hierarchy goes one step beyond what we've documented so far by introducing Operational Capability and Capability Contract as explicit layers between Architecture and Technology.

I believe these two layers are strategically important because they ensure that:

Architecture never directly depends on a library.
Technologies compete to satisfy capability contracts.
Workbench Architecture assembles technologies into one coherent operating system.
Components become implementation details rather than architectural units.

That hierarchy is, in my view, a defining characteristic of an institutional engineering platform and provides a durable framework for COREI's evolution over many years.
